ation than CHCl3/cosolvate systems. This step does not itself create proline, but it robustly achieves L-proline enrichment from racemic starting material.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify solvent/system (aqueous, ternary solution, etc.) for prebiotic relevance.\",\n        \"Reference the literature limits of amplification in the absence of cosolvents.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"best_pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n  \"overall_confidence\": 0.8,\n  \"network_feedback\": \"This network comprehensively and accurately encodes all plausible and literature-supported routes to proline, including speculated and detection-based branches, and is clear on environmental and mechanistic context. The explicit mapping of chiral amplification is commendable. Major weaknesses arise from gross atom and charge conservation violations in 17 of 21 reactions (including all critical cyanosulfidic and hydrolysis steps), principally due to unspecified byproducts and/or errors in product/reactant formulas. While these can often be remedied with explicit small-molecule additions, their current omission lowers the chemical feasibility and overall scientific reliability of the network. All speculative/hypothetical pathways are correctly flagged, and convective/atmospheric/environmental assignments are consistent with literature. Target achievement is robust, reaching L-proline through multiple plausible intermediates. To reach the 'excellent' threshold: (1) fix all mass and charge imbalances, (2) clarify speculative designations for undemonstrated steps, and (3) improve chemical detail for detection-based branches. As it stands, the network is good and holistic, but not yet excellent or fully valid until the conservation violations are resolved.\"\n}", "parsed_response": {"pathway_evaluations": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_001": 1.0, "rxn_002": 1.0, "rxn_003": 0.5, "rxn_005": 0.5, "rxn_006": 0.5, "rxn_0