ACIDS BY ULTRAVIOLET CIRCULARLY POLARIZED LIGHT IRRADIATION OF EXTRATERRESTRIAL ICE ANALOGS: A POSSIBLE SOURCE OF ASYMMETRY FOR PREBIOTIC CHEMISTRY - IOPscience (https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/0004-637X/788/1/79)\n  60. Shaping light in 3D for direct asymmetric photochemistry: a 1000-fold enantioselectivity enhancement (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-026-77096-w)\n  61. The Origin of Biological Homochirality - PMC (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6396334/)\n  62. (PDF) Rationalization and Prediction of Solution Enantiomeric Excess in Ternary Phase Systems (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/6808652_Rationalization_and_Prediction_of_Solution_Enantiomeric_Excess_in_Ternary_Phase_Systems)\n  63. Rationalization and Prediction of Solution Enantiomeric Excess in Ternary Phase Systems - Klussmann - 2006 - Angewandte Chemie International Edition - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/anie.200602520)\n  64. Amplification of Enantiomeric Excess in a Proline\u2010Mediated Reaction - Mathew - 2004 - Angewandte Chemie International Edition - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/anie.200453997)\n  65. The Origin of Biological Homochirality Donna G. Blackmond (https://cshperspectives.cshlp.org/content/11/3/a032540.full.pdf)\n  66. Chiral Sugars Drive Enantioenrichment in Prebiotic Amino Acid Synthesis (https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/acscentsci.7b00085)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.8\n- Feedback: This network comprehensively and accurately encodes all plausible and literature-supported routes to proline, including speculated and detection-based branches, and is clear on environmental and mechanistic context. The explicit mapping of chiral amplification is commendable. Major weaknesses arise from gross atom and charge conservation violations in 17 of 21 reactions (including all critical cyanosulfidic and hydrolysis steps), principally due to unspecified byproducts and/or errors in product/reactant formulas. While these can often be remedied with explicit small-molecule additions, their current omission lowers the chemical feasibility and overall scientific reliability of the network. All speculative/hypothetical pathways are correctly flagged, and convective/atmospheric/environmental assignments are consistent with literature. Target achievement is robust, reaching L-proline through multiple plausible intermediates. To reach the 'excellent' threshold: (1) fix all mass and charge imbalances, (2) clarify speculative designations for undemonstrated steps, and (3) improve chemical detail for detection-based branches. As it stands, the network is good and holistic, but not yet excellent or fully valid until the conservation violations are resolved.\n- Issues: ['This pathway describes a plausible cyanosulfidic branch to \u03b2-alanine (via 3-aminopropanal and \u03b2-aminopropionitrile), but suffers from the same balance issues as P1 for the photoreduction and hydrolysis steps. The steps are chemically reasonable as per the Patel et al. mechanism. Hydrolysis from prolinamide to proline remains problematic and unbalanced. Pathway coherence is good, but the terminal relevance to proline biosynthesis is lower.', \"This 'arginine branch' is chemically plausible up to the arginine aminonitrile, with guanidinylation supported by the literature. However, hydrolysis steps (rxn_014, 020 and 019) are unbalanced and lack experimental evidence for hydrothermal, nonenzymatic Arg/Orn\u2192Pro conversion. The pathway correctly reflects cyanamide as a competing branch; however, steps after arginine lack validation and violate mass/charge balance.\", \"This pathway reflects the detected presence of proline in extraterrestrial residue after hydrolysis, not a demonstrated abiotic route. Both steps are atom/charge unbalanced and mechanistically ill-defined: rxn_010 in particular does not conserve C/H/N, and proline must be released from unknown precursors rather than formed directly. This is a valid 'detection route,' but not a mechanistic synthesis.\", 'This spark discharge Strecker pathway reflects the literature detection of proline in Miller-type experiments. However, the steps are grossly unbalanced as written (rxn_011: not all C/H/N accounted for) and lack characterized intermediates specific to proline (e.g., pyrroline intermediates are only inferred). This aligns with literature as a detection route but does not constitute a valid stepwise synthesis.', 'This parent-body aqueous alteration route is literature accurate as a detection pathway, but the actual steps (rxn_012, rxn_008) are highly unbalanced and not mechanistically justified for proline. The route is included correctly as a detection outcome, but should not imply stepwise synthesis where none exists.', \"This speculative 'metabolism-first' pathway is correctly marked as hypothetical in the literature. Reductive amination of \u03b1-ketoglutarate to glutamate is shown experimentally under hydrothermal/mild aqueous conditions; however, all subsequent steps to proline (reduction and cyclization of GSA/P5C, reduction to proline) have not been demonstrated abiotically. Atom-balance is poor in all but the first step. The pathway's inclusion is valid for completeness but does not represent a feasible 