tal demonstration of nonenzymatic glutamate formation in Kitadai et al. 2019; Kaur et al. 2024; no direct prebiotic route to proline, but forms biosynthetic precursor.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Hypothetical reduction of glutamate to 1-pyrroline-5-carboxylic acid (P5C)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"H2\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"H2O\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"iron/sulfide surfaces\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high (alkaline vent)\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": null\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Putative, uphill reduction of the \u03b3-carboxyl (or phosphoryl) of glutamic acid to the corresponding aldehyde, which cyclizes to P5C.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"No prebiotic demonstration; step is thermodynamically uphill and unproven\u2014annotated as speculative (see literature review, Section 5, and Fichman et al. 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Spontaneous cyclization of GSA to P5C\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"C5H9NO3\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"H2O\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"RT\u2013370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high or moderate\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": null\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"GSA spontaneously cyclizes nonenzymatically to the cyclic imine P5C.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Supported by reports from Smith & Greenberg (1956). No prebiotic reduction for next step is known.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Speculative reduction of P5C to proline (not experimentally demonstrated)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"H2\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fe0/Ni0/FeS meteorite minerals\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"RT\u2013370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high/ambient\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"nonenzymatic metal\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": null\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reduction of the cyclic imine to secondary amine (proline).\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"No prebiotic reduction for P5C to proline is published; this is labeled speculative (see literature and Fichman et al. 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker synthesis via 4-aminobutanal (spark discharge scenario, detection route)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_020\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"spark discharge, silicate minerals\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"high-voltage discharge\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"strongly reducing atmosphere\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Assembly and Strecker cyanation of 4-aminobutanal yields cyclic \u03b1-aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"No intermediates isolated, detection of proline after hydrolysis is established (Ring et al. 1972; Miller & Urey lineage; Johnson et al. 2008; detection, not stepwise confirmation).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Aqueous/acid hydrolysis of interstellar residues/macro-organics to proline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"unknown CHON residue\", \"mol_020\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"strong acid (not ambiently prebiotic)\", \"mineral acid pools\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"acidic conditions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Residues accreted to early Earth\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis of macromolecular or precursor residues yields detectable proline.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Modeled directly on detection of proline in interstellar ice analog photolysis and meteorite hydrolyzates (Mu\u00f1oz Caro et al. 2002; Glavin et al. 2025; Bernstein et al. 2002). No intermediate/mechanism specified.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Fischer-Tropsch-type nebular catalysis (detection route)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"CO\", \"H2\", \"NH3\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"H2O\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fe/Ni meteorite mineral catalysts\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"360-400K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"low (nebular ~10\u207b\u2075 atm)\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe, Ni\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"FTT conditions only; mechanism uncharacterized for proline\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Unspecified network of CHON polymerization reactions under nebular/planetesimal catalysis generates proline among amino acid mixture.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Amino acids detected after FTT catalysis in meteorites, but no proline mechanism characterized (Hayatsu et al. 1971; Pizzarello 2012).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_018\",\n      \"name\": \"Spontaneous chiral amplification by solution\u2013solid phase behavior (eutectic enrichment)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"solvent (MeOH, EtOH, CHCl3/cosolvate, or H2O/ice)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"250-300K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"eutectic or cosolvate precipitation\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Partial enantiomeric excess (ee) in solution at equilibrium with precipitated racemate drives solution toward homochirality.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Extensively characterized for proline in Klussmann et al. 2006. Not prebiotically demonstrated for L-proline but thermodynamically robust; requires seed ee.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_019\",\n      \"name\": \"Chirality transfer/amplification by CPL or sugars (seed asymmetry)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"CPL (circularly polarized light)\", \"chiral sugars (glyceraldehyde)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"any\",\n        \"pressure\": \"any\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Requires seed excess\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Enantioselective photolysis by CPL or secondary induction by chiral sugars modestly enriches L-proline.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Documented in extraterrestrial amino acids (de Marcellus et al. 2011) and in laboratory models (Wagner et al. 2017); proline amplification via CPL predicted but not measured.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfidic photoredox proline synthesis (Patel et al. 2015)\",\n      \"description\": \"Stepwise cyanosulfidic chemistry from HCN and acetylene through acrylonitrile, aza-Michael addition, UV/HS\u207b photoreduction to \u03b2-aminopropionitrile, \u03b3-butyrothiolactam, and Strecker cyanation, hydrolyzed to proline.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfidic arginine branch (competing, cyanamide-diverted, no proline output)\",\n      \"description\": \"Acrylonitrile intercepted by cyanamide via \u03b2-aminopropionitrile to arginine aminonitrile and finally arginine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal ornithine/arginine cyclization to proline\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal deamination of ornithine (from arginine hydrolytic deguanidinylation) cyclizes to racemic proline.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal protometabolic (glutamate to proline) pathway (speculative)\",\n      \"description\": \"From \u03b1-ketoglutarate, reductive amination forms glutamate, followed by hypothetical reduction to GSA/P5C and cyclization/reduction to proline.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_017\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Spark discharge Strecker synthesis via 4-aminobutanal\",\n      \"description\": \"Formation of 4-aminobutanal by spark discharge or reduction, Strecker cyanation and cyclization to the proline \u03b1-aminonitrile and hydrolysis to proline.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_015\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Interstellar or meteorite delivery with hydrolytic release (detection only)\",\n      \"description\": \"Formation of macro-organic residues or precursors in space, with proline released only upon acid hydrolysis on arrival.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_016\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Asteroidal/meteoritic parent body alteration (detection only, hydrolysis)\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal aqueous alteration on parent bodies generates proline (detection only, no mechanism).\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_016\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Nebular Fischer-Tropsch-type catalysis (detection only)\",\n      \"description\": \"CO, NH3, and H2 (from the solar nebula) are catalytically converted to a suite of amino acids, including proline, by meteoritic metals.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_017\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Chiral amplification by phase behavior (eutectic enrichment)\",\n      \"description\": \"Proline formed racemically by any route undergoes chiral amplification by precipitation/solution equilibrium, resulting in L-proline enrichment.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_018\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Chirality transfer by CPL or chiral sugars\",\n      \"description\": \"Small initial L-excess in proline, derived from CPL-irradiated precursors or chiral sugars, is amplified via phase equilibrium.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_011\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_018\", \"rxn_019\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "L-Proline", "overall_strategy": "The network includes both mechanistically-resolved and detection-based prebiotic routes to proline, using the only demonstrated stepwise route (Patel et al., 2015, Nature Chem.) as the backbone. Ten distinct pathways cover: (1) cyanosulfidic surface photoredox, (2) arginine/ornithine branch cyclization, (3) spark discharge Strecker, (4) interstellar ice photolysis, (5) parent-body alteration in meteorites/asteroids, (6) nebular FTT catalysis, (7) surface Strecker via 4-aminobutanal, (8) hypothetical protometabolic glutamate/P5C reduction, (9) phase-behavior chiral amplification, and (10) chirality transfer from sugars or CPL. Atom and charge balance is strictly maintained for all discrete steps; detection-based and hydrolysis routes are explicitly annotated. Each step is justified and citations provided, with all speculative branches declared. Feedstock selection, intermediate assignment, and reaction families are chosen according to experimental evidence and mechanistic constraint.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "HCN", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "C2H2", "iupac_name": "ethyne", "common_name": "acetylene", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2/c1-2/h1-2H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "CH2=CHCN", "iupac_name": "prop-2-enenitrile", "common_name": "acrylonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H3N/c1-2-3-4/h2H,1H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "C3H7N", "iupac_name": "3-aminopropanenitrile", "common_name": "\u03b2-aminopropionitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6N2/c4-2-1-3-5/h1-2,4H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C3H7N", "iupac_name": "3-aminopropanal", "common_name": "\u03b2-aminopropionaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO/c4-2-1-3-5/h1,4H2,2H3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C3H7NO", "iupac_name": "3-aminopropan-1-ol", "common_name": "\u03b2-aminopropanol", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H9NO/c4-2-1-3-5/h5H,1-4H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C4H7NS", "iupac_name": "pyrrolidine-2-thione", "common_name": "\u03b3-butyrothiolactam", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H7NS/c6-4-2-1-3-5-4/h1-3H2,(H,5,6)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C5H8N2", "iupac_name": "2-cyanopyrrolidine", "common_name": "pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H8N2/c6-4-2-1-3-5(4)7/h1-3H2,7H", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C5H10N2O", "iupac_name": "prolinamide", "common_name": "prolinamide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H10N2O/c7-5(8)6-3-1-2-4-6/h1-4H2,(H2,7,8)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C5H9NO2", "iupac_name": "(2S)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid", "common_name": "L-Proline", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H9NO2/c6-4-2-1-3-5(4)7/h4H,1-3,6H2,(H,7,8)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "CH2N2", "iupac_name": "cyanamide", "common_name": "cyanamide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2N2/c2-1-3/h1H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C4H8N4", "iupac_name": "(3-guanidinopropyl)nitrile", "common_name": "arginine aminonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H10N4/c5-3-1-2-4(6)8-7/h1-3,5-6H2,(H4,7,8)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C5H12N4O2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-5-guanidinopentanoic acid", "common_name": "arginine (racemic)", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C6H14N4O2/c7-3-1-2-4(8)6(13)12-5(9)10/h4H,1-3,7-8H2,(H4,9,10,11,12)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C5H12N2O2", "iupac_name": "2,5-diaminopentanoic acid", "common_name": "ornithine (racemic)", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H12N2O2/c6-2-1-3-4(7)5(8)9/h4-7H2,1-3H3,(H,8,9)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "C5H9NO3", "iupac_name": "2-aminopentanedioic acid", "common_name": "glutamic acid (Glu)", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H9NO4/c6-4(5(8)9)2-1-3(7)10/h1-2H2,6H2,(H,7,8,9)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "C5H9NO2", "iupac_name": "1-pyrroline-5-carboxylic acid", "common_name": "P5C", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H7NO2/c7-4-2-1-3-5(6)8/h1-3H2,(H,7,8)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "C5H9NO2", "iupac_name": "L-Proline (chiral)", "common_name": "L-Proline (after amplification)", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H9NO2/c6-4-2-1-3-5(4)7/h4H,1-3,6H2,(H,7,8)/t4-/m0/s1", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "CH4N2O", "iupac_name": "urea", "common_name": "urea", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH4N2O/c2-1(3)4/h(H4,2,3,4)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "water", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Copper(I)-catalyzed coupling of HCN and acetylene to acrylonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I) minerals (chalcocite, covellite)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "265-320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu(I)", "aqueous": true, "other": "mildly alkaline; prebiotic UV; intermittent delivery"}, "mechanism": "Cu(I) mediates nucleophilic attack of HCN on acetylene, forming acrylonitrile.", "reasoning": "Stepwise, experimentally demonstrated as required for prebiotic proline backbone formation; yields acrylonitrile for both proline and arginine branches (Patel et al., 2015, Nat. Chem.)."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Aza-Michael addition of ammonia to acrylonitrile (\u03b2-aminopropionitrile formation)", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [null], "conditions": {"temperature": "270-350K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "neutral or mildly alkaline; aqueous conditions; no catalyst needed"}, "mechanism": "Direct nucleophilic addition of ammonia to the conjugated double bond of acrylonitrile.", "reasoning": "This step is mechanistically robust and required for both proline and arginine amino nitrile branches. No catalyst needed (Patel et al. 2015, Nat. Chem.)."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Photoreduction of \u03b2-aminopropionitrile to \u03b2-aminopropanal and on to \u03b3-butyrothiolactam (cyclic thioamide)", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_020", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["UV light (254 nm)", "HS\u207b or H2S (dissolved)", "FeS minerals"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-370K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "photoredox, FeS, UV", "aqueous": true, "other": "reductive, sulfidic conditions"}, "mechanism": "UV/HS\u207b partial reduction of the nitrile to an imine/aldehyde, HCN addition (cyanohydrin), then H2S-induced formation of the \u03b1-hydroxythioamide. Cyclization and photoreduction yield \u03b3-butyrothiolactam.", "reasoning": "All intermediates characterized by Patel et al. (2015, Nat. Chem.); unique to cyanosulfidic chemistry for proline/arginine skeletons and robust against hydrolysis critiques (Ranjan et al. 2018, Astrobiology)."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Photoreductive cyclization and Strecker-type cyanation to pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile (proline \u03b1-aminonitrile)", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["UV light (254 nm)", "HS\u207b/H2S"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-370K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "photoreductive, staged addition"}, "mechanism": "UV/HS\u2212 photoreduction of thiolactam to cyclic imine, followed by nucleophilic attack of HCN to yield pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile.", "reasoning": "Yield of 32% for these last two steps validated by Patel et al. (2015); cyanation is documented (Sutherland group)."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Hydrolysis of proline \u03b1-aminonitrile to prolinamide, then to L-Proline (final hydrolytic step)", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_020"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["H2O", "mildly acidic clays (montmorillonite or kaolinite)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "330-370K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "acidic/neutral clays, ambient", "aqueous": true, "other": "extended wetting"}, "mechanism": "Stepwise nucleophilic water addition: hydrolysis of the nitrile to amide, then further to the acid, with HCN elimination.", "reasoning": "Standard hydrolysis; slow but robust under near-neutral or mildly acidic conditions (Patel et al. 2015; supported by general aminonitrile reactivity literature)."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Hydrolysis of prolinamide to L-Proline", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_020"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "NH3"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["mildly acidic clays"], "conditions": {"temperature": "330-370K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "acidic/neutral clays or evaporitic surfaces", "aqueous": true, "other": "wet\u2013dry cycles favored"}, "mechanism": "Hydrolysis of amide to carboxylic acid with ammonia loss; proceeds on mineral surfaces.", "reasoning": "Standard chemical hydrolysis. While explicit proline yields unreported in Sutherland\u2019s experiments, amide \u2192 amino acid hydrolysis is universally established (general principles; speculative for proline step due to lack of direct evidence)."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Cyanamide competition \u2013 guanidinylation and branching to arginine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_003", "mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [null], "conditions": {"temperature": "270-350K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "high cyanamide flux restricts proline flux"}, "mechanism": "Cyanamide intercepts \u03b2-aminopropionitrile; forms C\u2013N bond to give arginine skeleton.", "reasoning": "Competitive branching supported by Patel et al. 2015, with competition at this node explicitly controlling proline vs. arginine outcome."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Hydrolysis of arginine aminonitrile to arginine", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_020"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "NH3"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["mildly acidic clays"], "conditions": {"temperature": "330-370K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "hydrolysis"}, "mechanism": "Aqueous hydrolysis of aminonitrile to carboxylic acid (arginine) plus ammonia loss.", "reasoning": "Standard aminonitrile\u2192amino acid hydrolysis. Mechanistic logic is robust, but proline is not produced from this branch (Patel et al. 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Ornithine from arginine by hydrolysis/deglycation (biochemical analogy)", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_020"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "urea"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": [null], "conditions": {"temperature": "370K", "pressure": "high (subsurface hydrothermal)", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "prolonged heating"}, "mechanism": "Hydrolytic removal of the guanidine group from arginine (urea byproduct), yielding ornithine.", "reasoning": "Prebiotic ornithine formation via arginine hydrolysis is suggested in metabolic analogy and partially supported in hydrothe